---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { LIVE_TOOLS } from '../lib/tools';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Why UtilDock exists: small, fast, ad-free developer utilities that run entirely in your browser."
>
  <article class="ud-prose mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(1.9rem,4.5vw,3rem)] text-chalk">About {SITE.name}</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-temper">{SITE.tagline}</p>

    <div class="mt-10 space-y-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-temper">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-chalk">Why this exists</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Every developer needs to pretty-print some JSON or work out why two payloads disagree. The
          tools that come up first for those searches tend to be buried in ads, interrupted by cookie
          walls, and — most uncomfortably — they often post whatever you paste to a server you know
          nothing about. That is a bad trade when the thing you are debugging is a production
          payload.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          {SITE.name} is the opposite of that: a small set of sharp tools, no advertising, no
          sign-up, and no data leaving your machine.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-chalk">How it is built</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The site is static — HTML, CSS and a little JavaScript, served from Cloudflare's edge. Each
          tool is a self-contained component that hydrates only on its own page, so the pages you are
          not using cost you nothing. The heavy work (parsing, validating, diffing) happens in a Web
          Worker, which is why a multi-megabyte document does not freeze the page while you type.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          There is no backend. That is a deliberate constraint: a tool that cannot send your data
          anywhere cannot leak it. Read the <a href="/privacy" class="text-cherry hover:underline"
            >privacy page</a
          > for the details.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-chalk">What is here today</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
          {
            LIVE_TOOLS.map((tool) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={tool.href}
                  class="flex items-center gap-2.5 border border-scribe bg-anvil px-3 py-2.5 text-sm text-chalk transition-colors hover:border-scribe-strong hover:bg-anvil-lit"
                >
                  <Icon name={tool.icon} size={16} class="shrink-0 text-cherry" />
                  {tool.name}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4">
          JSON came first because it is what most of us reach for most often. JWT decoding is next,
          and the list will keep growing — always under the same rules: fast, free, ad-free, and
          entirely client-side.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
